//! Round trips of Discord's documented example payloads through the typed
//! models (3.0 series 3 contract, docs/design/3.0-types-events-requests.md
//! §2 "시리즈 3 결과").
//!
//! Every model whose discriminants became open newtypes must still decode the
//! examples in discord-api-docs `3714862` ([`payloads`], regenerated by
//! `ci/doc_examples.py`) and write them back as equivalent JSON:
//!
//! - every key the model writes was in the example with the same value
//!   (numbers stay numbers, permission strings stay strings), except keys the
//!   model always writes with an empty default (`[]`, `false`, ...);
//! - no discriminant key of a modeled object is dropped
//!   ([`DISCRIMINANT_KEYS`]);
//! - decoding the written JSON again writes the same JSON.
//!
//! The second half pins the open-value contract per converted field: an
//! unknown value that fits the storage round-trips unchanged and one past the
//! storage width is a decode error, not a truncation.
//!
//! It also pins the received modal values (a later fix, same payloads): a
//! submitted checkbox's boolean `value`, a radio group's string `value`, and
//! the `values` lists, both through `MessageComponent` and the modal parser.
//!
//! The interaction tests (phase 4 direct decoding) send every documented
//! interaction — the command interactions, the message component
//! interactions, and the modal submits — through
//! [`crate::decode_interaction`] in Discord's key order and with `data`
//! moved first, and write them back through the wire-format `Serialize`.

// Generated verbatim by ci/doc_examples.py; formatting it would make the
// next regeneration a noisy diff.
#[rustfmt::skip]
mod payloads;

use serde::de::DeserializeOwned;
use serde::Serialize;
use serde_json::{json, Value};

